---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import HEICConverterPNG from '../components/HEICConverterPNG.astro';
import SEOContentHEIC from '../components/SEOContentHEIC.astro';

const SEO_TITLE = "Convert HEIC to PNG — Free Local Image Converter";
const SEO_DESC = "Convert Apple iPhone HEIC and HEIF photos to high-quality PNG format. 100% free, completely secure, and processed entirely offline on your device.";
---

<Layout title={SEO_TITLE} description={SEO_DESC}>
  <style is:global>
    :root {
      --color-lime: #0088ff; /* Using the format converter accent color */
    }
  </style>

  <section class="relative z-20 pt-20 container-stripe">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; background: rgba(0, 136, 255, 0.08); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 136, 255, 0.2); border-radius: 100px; padding: 5px 14px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--color-lime);">Zero-Server Converter</span>
      </div>

      <h1 style="font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, 4.5rem); line-height: 1; margin: 0 0 24px; letter-spacing: -0.05em;">
        Convert HEIC <br/>
        <span style="color: var(--color-lime);">to PNG.</span>
      </h1>
      <p style="color: var(--color-ink-2); font-size: 1.125rem; margin-bottom: 24px; max-width: 500px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; line-height: 1.6;">
        Instantly transform your iPhone photos into lossless PNG format. Processed 100% securely on your local device.
      </p>
    </div>

    <HEICConverterPNG />
  </section>

  <SEOContentHEIC toolName="convert-heic-to-png" />

  <!-- Comprehensive Technical & Workflow Guide -->
  <article style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 80px auto 120px; padding: 48px 24px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); font-family: var(--font-body); line-height: 1.8; color: var(--color-ink-2);">
    
    <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.25rem); font-weight: 800; color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 24px; letter-spacing: -0.03em; line-height: 1.2;">
      Understanding HEIC to PNG Conversion: Fidelity, Transparency, and Professional Workflows
    </h2>

    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px; font-size: 1.0625rem;">
      Since the rollout of iOS 11 and macOS High Sierra, Apple adopted the High Efficiency Image Container (<strong>HEIC</strong> / <strong>HEIF</strong>) as its default photographic storage standard. Powered by the High Efficiency Video Coding (HEVC / H.265) compression algorithm, HEIC allows modern iPhones (from iPhone 7 up to the latest iPhone 16 Pro) to capture richly detailed 12-megapixel and 48-megapixel images at roughly half the file size of legacy JPEGs. However, outside the closed Apple hardware ecosystem, HEIC remains notoriously difficult to handle. Windows workstations, Linux distributions, older image editing suites, and institutional Indian portals routinely refuse to open or accept <code>.heic</code> files.
    </p>

    <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
      When bridging this compatibility gap, users face a fundamental decision: convert to <strong>JPG</strong> or convert to <strong>PNG</strong>. While JPG is often chosen for casual snapshots due to its small footprint, <strong>PNG (Portable Network Graphics)</strong> is the undisputed standard whenever image fidelity, sharp typographic boundaries, digital signatures, or transparent alpha channels must be preserved without compromise.
    </p>

    <!-- Callout Box: Summary of Key Differences -->
    <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 16px; padding: 28px; margin: 36px 0;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 16px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;">
        <span style="display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-lime);"></span>
        Core Reasons to Choose PNG Over JPG for HEIC Files
      </h3>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 20px; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));">
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;">1. True Lossless Reconstruction</strong>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem;">PNG relies on DEFLATE compression. It decodes the raw bitmap from HEIC and stores every single RGB pixel value without discarding data or adding second-generation compression noise.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;">2. Full 8-Bit Alpha Transparency</strong>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem;">JPEG cannot store transparent pixels. If your HEIC contains an isolated subject, cutout sticker, or transparent graphic, only PNG maintains a clean, transparent background.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;">3. Razor-Sharp Linework & Typography</strong>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem;">Screenshots, digital signatures, official stamps, and diagrams avoid the blurry "haloing" and blocky artifacts that plague JPEG compression.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;">4. 100% Universal Compatibility</strong>
          <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem;">Unlike HEIC, which requires proprietary codec licenses on Windows, PNG is supported natively across every browser, desktop OS, mobile platform, and design app.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Lossless vs Lossy -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
      When to Choose PNG Over JPG: Avoiding Generation Loss
    </h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      To appreciate the engineering advantage of PNG, one must understand how digital compression behaves over multiple iterations. Standard iPhone HEIC photos are already compressed using lossy HEVC intra-coding. When you convert that HEIC photo into a standard JPG, you subject the image to a second round of lossy Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) quantization. This phenomenon is known in digital signal processing as <em>generation loss</em>.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      During generation loss, subtle color gradations become stepped (color banding), high-frequency details (such as eyelashes, fabric textures, fine text, and biometric line patterns) are smoothed out, and high-contrast edges develop "mosquito noise." In contrast, <strong>PNG uses lossless DEFLATE compression</strong> (RFC 1951, combining LZ77 substitution with Huffman tree coding). The browser decodes the HEIC into an uncompressed raster bitmap in memory, and PNG compresses that bitmap without discarding a single subpixel of data. What you see in the original iPhone rendering is mathematically preserved bit-for-bit in the resulting PNG.
    </p>

    <!-- Section: Graphic Designers Workflow -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
      Graphic Designers and the Modern HEIC to PNG Pipeline
    </h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      For digital artists, UI/UX designers, marketing production teams, and front-end engineers, iPhone mobile photography has become an indispensable source of raw assets. Clients regularly shoot product samples, event banners, team portraits, texture references, and site inspection photos using modern iPhones and deliver them as raw <code>.heic</code> attachments.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      When working in collaborative environments like <strong>Figma</strong>, <strong>Adobe Illustrator</strong>, <strong>Canva</strong>, or <strong>Affinity Photo</strong>, dropping an unprocessed HEIC file onto the design canvas often results in an unsupported file error. While Windows users can purchase an optional codec extension from the Microsoft Store, cross-functional teams cannot rely on every stakeholder having local HEIF decoders installed. Converting these assets to PNG before importing solves three major creative roadblocks:
    </p>
    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px;">
      <li><strong>Chroma Subsampling Integrity:</strong> Standard JPEGs commonly compress color data using 4:2:0 chrominance subsampling, discarding up to 75% of color resolution compared to luminance. PNG maintains full 4:4:4 color representation across 24-bit Truecolor (8 bits each for Red, Green, and Blue), ensuring brand logos, UI mockups, and client color palettes remain vibrant and unshifted.</li>
      <li><strong>Layer Masking & Alpha Channel Preservation:</strong> On iOS 16 and later, Apple's Visual Look Up feature allows users to tap and hold the subject of a photo to instantly lift it from the background as a cutout sticker. If exported to JPG, that transparent silhouette is irreversibly flattened onto an opaque white or black box. Converting the cutout to PNG preserves the 8-bit alpha channel, giving designers a ready-to-composite asset for websites, presentations, and social media collaterals.</li>
      <li><strong>Vector-Ready Tracing:</strong> Scanned logo sketches, handwritten signatures, and schematic drawings photographed on an iPhone convert into ultra-sharp PNGs that vectorization engines (such as Illustrator Image Trace) can outline without introducing false anchor points from compression noise.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Section: macOS Screenshots & iPhone Photos -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
      macOS Screenshots and iPhone Cross-Device Workflow
    </h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Power users working across macOS (Sonoma, Sequoia) and iOS frequently sync screenshots and captures via AirDrop, iCloud Shared Albums, or local cable sync. Depending on system settings and capture utilities, high-resolution screenshots of development consoles, database records, financial statements, and UI bug captures can end up saved or encapsulated in HEIF format.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      When sharing these screenshots with colleagues on Windows PCs, Linux workstations, or bug-tracking software like <strong>Jira</strong>, <strong>Linear</strong>, or <strong>GitHub Issues</strong>, HEIC files fail to display inline. Converting screenshots to PNG guarantees that monospaced code fonts, small 10pt interface labels, subtle 1px border lines, and dark-mode terminal outputs remain crisp and legible. In screenshot documentation, a JPG's blurry compression halo around letters is not just cosmetically poor—it can render critical error codes and IP addresses illegible. PNG completely eliminates this issue.
    </p>

    <!-- Comparison Table -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
      Technical Comparison: Apple HEIC vs Lossless PNG vs Standard JPG
    </h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Here is how the three predominant image formats compare across critical technical specifications:
    </p>

    <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 28px 0; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; background: var(--color-surface-1);">
      <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <th style="padding: 14px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--color-ink);">Parameter</th>
            <th style="padding: 14px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--color-ink);">Apple HEIC</th>
            <th style="padding: 14px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--color-lime);">Lossless PNG</th>
            <th style="padding: 14px 16px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--color-ink);">Standard JPG</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Compression Scheme</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Lossy (HEVC / H.265)</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 600;">Lossless (DEFLATE / LZ77)</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Lossy (DCT Quantization)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Alpha Transparency</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Supported (Auxiliary channels)</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 600;">Full 8-bit Alpha (256 levels)</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; color: #ef4444;">Not Supported (0 levels)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Generation Loss</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Initial capture encoding</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 600;">Zero (Bit-for-bit exact)</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; color: #ef4444;">Cumulative artifacting</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Typical Size (12MP Photo)</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">1.5 MB – 3.2 MB</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">6.0 MB – 18.0 MB</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">1.2 MB – 2.8 MB</td>
          </tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Browser Support</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Safari & Apple OS only</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 600;">100% Universal (All browsers)</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">100% Universal (All browsers)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">Ideal Application</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Apple camera storage</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px; color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 600;">Design, UI, text, cutouts, signatures</td>
            <td style="padding: 12px 16px;">Web photography, social feeds</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: File Size Considerations -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
      File Size Considerations: Understanding Why PNG Files Are Larger
    </h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      A frequent question from users converting HEIC to PNG is: <em>"Why did my 2 MB iPhone photo turn into a 10 MB PNG file?"</em>
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      This increase is entirely natural and is a direct consequence of how PNG handles image data. HEIC uses predictive inter-frame and intra-frame block encoding derived from modern video codecs, intelligently throwing away microscopic frequencies that human eyes barely detect. JPG does something similar using frequency quantization tables.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      PNG, by design, discards zero pixel data. An uncompressed 12-megapixel photograph (4032 x 3024 pixels) represents approximately 36 million bytes (36.5 MB) of raw RGBA pixel data in RAM. PNG's pre-compression filters and DEFLATE algorithm compress that raw 36.5 MB block down to roughly 8 MB to 14 MB without losing a single bit of information. While this makes PNG larger than HEIC or JPG, it delivers uncompromising archival quality.
    </p>
    <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-lime); padding: 20px 24px; border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0; margin: 28px 0;">
      <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;">Need to meet strict file size limits?</strong>
      <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
        If an institutional portal or exam form specifically requires PNG format but caps the maximum upload size (e.g. at 100 KB, 200 KB, or 500 KB), you can convert your HEIC file to PNG using this tool, and then pass the resulting PNG through our free <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb" style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">100KB image compressor</a> or <a href="/compress-image-to-200kb" style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">200KB compressor</a> to match the exact byte threshold.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Government & Institutional Portals -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
      When Indian Government Portals & Official Forms Specifically Require PNG
    </h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      In India, government recruitment boards, professional certification bodies, and legal registries handle millions of digital document uploads every year. While common examination portals (such as UPSC, SSC CGL, and IBPS) primarily ask for JPEG photographs, an increasing number of statutory, corporate, and educational portals explicitly require or prefer <strong>PNG format</strong> for specific document categories:
    </p>

    <div style="display: grid; gap: 24px; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(290px, 1fr)); margin: 28px 0;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: block; font-size: 1.0625rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">MCA21 & SPICe+ (Corporate Affairs)</strong>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
          When incorporating a private limited company or filing Form DIR-3 for a Director Identification Number (DIN), scanned specimen signatures and company seals must be uploaded. The Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA21 v3) portal requires sharp, high-contrast signatures in PNG or JPG format (often 10 KB – 50 KB) to ensure digital verification algorithms can detect stroke authenticity without pixel distortion.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: block; font-size: 1.0625rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">Income Tax & GST Common Portal (GSTN)</strong>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
          Under GST REG-01 (New GST Registration) and Income Tax Form 15CA/CB, authorized signatories must provide signature scans and partnership deed stamps. PNG format is strongly recommended by tax practitioners because black ink on white paper remains crisp and free from compression smudges that trigger manual officer scrutiny or rejection.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: block; font-size: 1.0625rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">EPFO Member e-Sewa & Joint Declarations</strong>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
          Updating employee profiles, EPS-95 pension nominations, and employer signature authorizations on the EPFO unified portal frequently specifies clear scanned images in PNG or PDF format. Blurry JPEG compression often renders rubber stamp seals illegible, causing lengthy claim rejections.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); display: block; font-size: 1.0625rem; margin-bottom: 8px;">State PSCs & University Admissions (CUET / DU CSAS)</strong>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem;">
          State recruitment portals such as MPSC, TNPSC, and WBPSC, alongside centralized university portals like Delhi University CSAS and CUET (NTA), accept signature uploads in PNG format. Candidates capturing their signed white paper using an iPhone find their files saved as <code>.heic</code>, which results in instant rejection errors when uploaded. Converting to PNG ensures immediate acceptance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
      <strong>Why do Indian official portals fail when uploading HEIC directly?</strong> Most government backends operate on legacy enterprise stacks—such as Java J2EE with Apache Tomcat, Oracle WebLogic, or Microsoft IIS with older .NET frameworks. These platforms lack the licensed HEVC/libheif codecs required to parse HEIC files. When a candidate uploads a raw HEIC file from their iPhone, the server either throws a generic <em>"Invalid image format. Only JPG, JPEG, and PNG allowed"</em> warning or crashes the upload session entirely. Converting HEIC to standard PNG on your local device guarantees seamless acceptance across every portal.
    </p>

    <!-- Section: Batch Conversion & Privacy -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
      Zero-Server Architecture & High-Speed Batch Conversion
    </h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Online file converters often pose a substantial security and privacy hazard. Traditional conversion services upload your images to third-party cloud servers where they are queued, processed, and stored on remote storage buckets. For individuals handling sensitive identity documents—such as Aadhaar cards, PAN cards, voter identification, university transcripts, or personal family photographs—cloud uploads carry an inherent risk of data exposure.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      <strong>PdfAndImg eliminates this risk entirely through our Zero-Server architecture.</strong> Our HEIC to PNG tool executes entirely within your browser's local sandbox using client-side <strong>WebAssembly (Wasm)</strong>. When you select or drag HEIC files into the upload box:
    </p>
    <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px;">
      <li><strong>0% Cloud Transmission:</strong> Your files are read directly into your device's local memory (RAM). Not a single byte is uploaded to any remote server or external API.</li>
      <li><strong>Local Multi-Threaded Decoding:</strong> The WebAssembly decoder unpacks the HEIC container locally on your CPU or GPU, converting raw image vectors into standard raster pixels.</li>
      <li><strong>High-Speed Batch Support:</strong> You can select 5, 10, or 20 HEIC files simultaneously. The converter processes the batch sequentially or in parallel without queue delays or throttling, allowing you to download every converted PNG with a single click.</li>
      <li><strong>Offline Functionality:</strong> Once this page is loaded in your browser, you can disconnect your Wi-Fi or mobile data and continue converting HEIC files to PNG without an active internet connection.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Section: Step-by-Step Instructions -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 48px; margin-bottom: 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
      Step-by-Step: How to Convert HEIC to PNG on Your Device
    </h3>
    <ol style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 28px 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 16px; align-items: flex-start;">
        <span style="flex-shrink: 0; width: 32px; height: 32px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-surface-3); border: 1px solid var(--color-border-mid); display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-lime);">1</span>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); font-size: 1.0625rem; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">Select Your HEIC Files</strong>
          <span style="font-size: 0.9375rem;">Click the "Browse files" button above or drag and drop one or multiple <code>.heic</code> or <code>.heif</code> images directly into the converter drop zone.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 16px; align-items: flex-start;">
        <span style="flex-shrink: 0; width: 32px; height: 32px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-surface-3); border: 1px solid var(--color-border-mid); display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-lime);">2</span>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); font-size: 1.0625rem; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">Confirm File Selection</strong>
          <span style="font-size: 0.9375rem;">The tool displays the filename and file count. You can verify your chosen images or click "Change" to select a different batch.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 16px; align-items: flex-start;">
        <span style="flex-shrink: 0; width: 32px; height: 32px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-surface-3); border: 1px solid var(--color-border-mid); display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-lime);">3</span>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); font-size: 1.0625rem; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">Execute Local Conversion</strong>
          <span style="font-size: 0.9375rem;">Click the blue "Convert to PNG" button. The WebAssembly engine decodes the HEVC payload into uncompressed bitmaps and compiles them into lossless PNG files.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li style="display: flex; gap: 16px; align-items: flex-start;">
        <span style="flex-shrink: 0; width: 32px; height: 32px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-surface-3); border: 1px solid var(--color-border-mid); display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-lime);">4</span>
        <div>
          <strong style="color: var(--color-ink); font-size: 1.0625rem; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">Instant Lossless Download</strong>
          <span style="font-size: 0.9375rem;">Once conversion completes, download links are generated instantly. Save your brand-new PNG files directly to your hard drive or mobile storage.</span>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <!-- Custom Tool-Specific FAQs -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin-top: 56px; margin-bottom: 24px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
      Frequently Asked Questions (HEIC to PNG)
    </h3>
    
    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 28px; margin-bottom: 40px;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 10px;">
          1. Why does my iPhone capture photos in HEIC instead of standard PNG or JPG?
        </h4>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.7;">
          Apple switched to the HEIC (High Efficiency Image Container) format starting with iOS 11 to solve mobile storage constraints. Modern iPhone sensors capture immense detail (especially on 48MP Pro models), and storing them in uncompressed PNG or legacy JPEG would quickly fill device memory. HEIC leverages the advanced HEVC (H.265) video codec to achieve roughly 50% better compression than JPEG while supporting 16-bit color depth, transparency, and burst sequences. However, because it requires proprietary licensing, non-Apple ecosystems have lagged in supporting it natively.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 10px;">
          2. Will converting HEIC to PNG improve or enhance the original photo quality?
        </h4>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.7;">
          No converter can add information that was not present in the original capture. However, converting to PNG guarantees <strong>zero loss of quality</strong> from the source file. Unlike converting to JPG—which applies a second layer of lossy compression that degrades details and introduces artifacting—PNG is mathematically lossless. It decodes the exact raster pixels rendered by your iPhone and preserves them bit-for-bit, ensuring maximum visual fidelity for editing, printing, and official archival.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 10px;">
          3. Why is the converted PNG file significantly larger than the original HEIC file?
        </h4>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.7;">
          HEIC is an advanced lossy compression format optimized for maximum compression ratios, whereas PNG is an uncompressed or losslessly compressed bitmap format. When a 2.5 MB HEIC photo is converted to PNG, the browser decodes every individual pixel into 24-bit Truecolor RGBA channels. PNG compresses this data without discarding high-frequency detail. As a result, the resulting PNG is typically 3 to 5 times larger than the HEIC file, reflecting its true uncompromised pixel fidelity.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 10px;">
          4. Can I convert transparent iPhone stickers and subject cutouts to transparent PNG?
        </h4>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.7;">
          Yes! iOS features like "Visual Look Up" allow you to tap and isolate a person, pet, or object from a photograph background. When these cutouts are saved or exported as HEIC images with auxiliary alpha masks, our converter extracts the alpha transparency layer and encodes it directly into a standard 32-bit RGBA PNG. This allows you to drop the transparent graphic into Figma, Photoshop, Canva, or website code without any white background box.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
        <h4 style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 10px;">
          5. Is there a limit on how many HEIC files I can batch convert at once?
        </h4>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.7;">
          Because all conversion happens locally on your own machine's hardware using WebAssembly and client-side RAM, there are no artificial server quotas or paywalls. You can upload batches of 10, 20, or more photos at once. Processing speed depends entirely on your device's processor speed and available RAM. For best performance when processing 48MP ProRAW/HEIC files, we recommend converting in batches of 10 to 15 files at a time.
        </p>
